@NODE S1 "14.1. roman.py, stage 1"
@TITLE "14.1. roman.py, stage 1"
@NEXT "S2"
@PREV "S0"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}14.1. @{code}roman.py@{body}, stage 1 @{ub}

Now that the unit tests are complete, it's time to start writing the code that the test cases are attempting to test. You're going to do this in stages, so you can see all the unit tests fail, then watch them pass one by one as you fill in the gaps in @{code}roman.py@{body}.

@{b}Example 14.1. @{code}roman1.py@{body}@{ub}

This file is available in @{code}py/roman/stage1/@{body} in the examples directory.

If you have not already done so, you can download this and other examples used in this book.

@{code}

"""Convert to and from Roman numerals"""

#Define exceptions
class RomanError(Exception): pass
class OutOfRangeError(RomanError): pass
class NotIntegerError(RomanError): pass
class InvalidRomanNumeralError(RomanError): pass

def toRoman(n):
"""convert integer to Roman numeral"""
pass

def fromRoman(s):
"""convert Roman numeral to integer"""
pass

@{body}

This is how you define your own custom exceptions in Python. Exceptions are classes, and you create your own by subclassing existing exceptions. It is strongly recommended (but not required) that you subclass @{code}Exception@{body}, which is the base class that all built-in exceptions inherit from. Here I am defining @{code}RomanError@{body} (inherited from @{code}Exception@{body}) to act as the base class for all my other custom exceptions to follow. This is a matter of style; I could just as easily have inherited each individual exception from the @{code}Exception@{body} class directly.

The @{code}OutOfRangeError@{body} and @{code}NotIntegerError@{body} exceptions will eventually be used by @{code}toRoman@{body} to flag various forms of invalid input, as specified in @{code}ToRomanBadInput@{body}.

The @{code}InvalidRomanNumeralError@{body} exception will eventually be used by @{code}fromRoman@{body} to flag invalid input, as specified in @{code}FromRomanBadInput@{body}.

At this stage, you want to define the API of each of your functions, but you don't want to code them yet, so you stub them out using the Python reserved word @{code}pass@{body}.

Now for the big moment (drum roll please): you're finally going to run the unit test against this stubby little module. At this point, every test case should fail. In fact, if any test case passes in stage 1, you should go back to @{code}romantest.py@{body} and re-evaluate why you coded a test so useless that it passes with do-nothing functions.

Run @{code}romantest1.py@{body} with the @{code}-v@{body} command-line option, which will give more verbose output so you can see exactly what's going on as each test case runs. With any luck, your output should look like this:

@{b}Example 14.2. Output of @{code}romantest1.py@{body} against @{code}roman1.py@{body}@{ub}

