@{body}

Running the script runs @{code}unittest.main()@{body}, which runs each test case, which is to say each method defined in each class within @{code}romantest.py@{body}. For each test case, it prints out the @{code}doc string@{body} of the method and whether that test passed or failed. As expected, none of the test cases passed.

For each failed test case, @{code}unittest@{body} displays the trace information showing exactly what happened. In this case, the call to @{code}assertRaises@{body} (also called @{code}failUnlessRaises@{body}) raised an @{code}AssertionError@{body} because it was expecting @{code}toRoman@{body} to raise an @{code}OutOfRangeError@{body} and it didn't.

After the detail, @{code}unittest@{body} displays a summary of how many tests were performed and how long it took.

Overall, the unit test failed because at least one test case did not pass. When a test case doesn't pass, @{code}unittest@{body} distinguishes between failures and errors. A failure is a call to an @{code}assertXYZ@{body} method, like @{code}assertEqual@{body} or @{code}assertRaises@{body}, that fails because the asserted condition is not true or the expected exception was not raised. An error is any other sort of exception raised in the code you're testing or the unit test case itself. For instance, the @{code}testFromRomanCase@{body} method ("@{code}fromRoman@{body} should only accept uppercase input") was an error, because the call to @{code}numeral.upper()@{body} raised an @{code}AttributeError@{body} exception, because @{code}toRoman@{body} was supposed to return a string but didn't. But @{code}testZero@{body} ("@{code}toRoman@{body} should fail with 0 input") was a failure, because the call to @{code}fromRoman@{body} did not raise the @{code}InvalidRomanNumeral@{body} exception that @{code}assertRaises@{body} was looking for.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S2 "14.2. roman.py, stage 2"
@TITLE "14.2. roman.py, stage 2"
@NEXT "S3"
@PREV "S1"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}14.2. @{code}roman.py@{body}, stage 2 @{ub}

Now that you have the framework of the @{code}roman@{body} module laid out, it's time to start writing code and passing test cases.

@{b}Example 14.3. @{code}roman2.py@{body}@{ub}

This file is available in @{code}py/roman/stage2/@{body} in the examples directory.

If you have not already done so, you can download this and other examples used in this book.

@{code}

"""Convert to and from Roman numerals"""

#Define exceptions
class RomanError(Exception): pass
class OutOfRangeError(RomanError): pass
class NotIntegerError(RomanError): pass
class InvalidRomanNumeralError(RomanError): pass

#Define digit mapping
romanNumeralMap = (('M', 1000),
('CM', 900),
('D', 500),
('CD', 400),
('C', 100),
('XC', 90),
('L', 50),
('XL', 40),
('X', 10),
('IX', 9),
('V', 5),
('IV', 4),
('I', 1))

def toRoman(n):
"""convert integer to Roman numeral"""
result = ""
for numeral, integer in romanNumeralMap:
while n >= integer:
result += numeral
n -= integer
return result

def fromRoman(s):
"""convert Roman numeral to integer"""
pass

@{body}

@{code}romanNumeralMap@{body} is a tuple of tuples which defines three things:

* The character representations of the most basic Roman numerals. Note that this is not just the single-character Roman numerals; you're also defining two-character pairs like @{code}CM@{body} ("one hundred less than one thousand"); this will make the @{code}toRoman@{body} code simpler later.

* The order of the Roman numerals. They are listed in descending value order, from @{code}M@{body} all the way down to @{code}I@{body}.

* The value of each Roman numeral. Each inner tuple is a pair of @{code}(@{i}numeral@{ui}, @{i}value@{ui})@{body}.

Here's where your rich data structure pays off, because you don't need any special logic to handle the subtraction rule. To convert to Roman numerals, you simply iterate through @{code}romanNumeralMap@{body} looking for the largest integer value less than or equal to the input. Once found, you add the Roman numeral representation to the end of the output, subtract the corresponding integer value from the input, lather, rinse, repeat.

@{b}Example 14.4. How @{code}toRoman@{body} works @{ub}

If you're not clear how @{code}toRoman@{body} works, add a @{code}print@{body} statement to the end of the @{code}while@{body} loop:

@{code}

while n >= integer:
result += numeral
n -= integer
print 'subtracting', integer, 'from input, adding', numeral, 'to output'
@{body}

@{code}

>>> import roman2
>>> roman2.toRoman(1424)
subtracting 1000 from input, adding M to output
subtracting 400 from input, adding CD to output
subtracting 10 from input, adding X to output
subtracting 10 from input, adding X to output
subtracting 4 from input, adding IV to output
'MCDXXIV'

@{body}

So @{code}toRoman@{body} appears to work, at least in this manual spot check. But will it pass the unit testing? Well no, not entirely.

@{b}Example 14.5. Output of @{code}romantest2.py@{body} against @{code}roman2.py@{body}@{ub}

Remember to run @{code}romantest2.py@{body} with the @{code}-v@{body} command-line flag to enable verbose mode.

@{code}
fromRoman should only accept uppercase input ... FAIL
toRoman should always return uppercase ... ok
fromRoman should fail with malformed antecedents ... FAIL
fromRoman should fail with repeated pairs of numerals ... FAIL
fromRoman should fail with too many repeated numerals ... FAIL
fromRoman should give known result with known input ... FAIL
toRoman should give known result with known input ... ok
fromRoman(toRoman(n))==n for all n ... FAIL
toRoman should fail with non-integer input ... FAIL
toRoman should fail with negative input ... FAIL
toRoman should fail with large input ... FAIL
toRoman should fail with 0 input ... FAIL
@{body}

@{code}toRoman@{body} does, in fact, always return uppercase, because @{code}romanNumeralMap@{body} defines the Roman numeral representations as uppercase. So this test passes already.

Here's the big news: this version of the @{code}toRoman@{body} function passes the known values test. Remember, it's not comprehensive, but it does put the function through its paces with a variety of good inputs, including inputs that produce every single-character Roman numeral, the largest possible input (@{code}3999@{body}), and the input that produces the longest possible Roman numeral (@{code}3888@{body}). At this point, you can be reasonably confident that the function works for any good input value you could throw at it.

However, the function does not "work" for bad values; it fails every single bad input test. That makes sense, because you didn't include any checks for bad input. Those test cases look for specific exceptions to be raised (via @{code}assertRaises@{body}), and you're never raising them. You'll do that in the next stage.

Here's the rest of the output of the unit test, listing the details of all the failures. You're down to 10.

