@SMARTWRAP

@{b}14.4. @{code}roman.py@{body}, stage 4 @{ub}

Now that @{code}toRoman@{body} is done, it's time to start coding @{code}fromRoman@{body}. Thanks to the rich data structure that maps individual Roman numerals to integer values, this is no more difficult than the @{code}toRoman@{body} function.

@{b}Example 14.9. @{code}roman4.py@{body}@{ub}

This file is available in @{code}py/roman/stage4/@{body} in the examples directory.

If you have not already done so, you can download this and other examples used in this book.

@{code}

"""Convert to and from Roman numerals"""

#Define exceptions
class RomanError(Exception): pass
class OutOfRangeError(RomanError): pass
class NotIntegerError(RomanError): pass
class InvalidRomanNumeralError(RomanError): pass

#Define digit mapping
romanNumeralMap = (('M', 1000),
('CM', 900),
('D', 500),
('CD', 400),
('C', 100),
('XC', 90),
('L', 50),
('XL', 40),
('X', 10),
('IX', 9),
('V', 5),
('IV', 4),
('I', 1))

# toRoman function omitted for clarity (it hasn't changed)

def fromRoman(s):
"""convert Roman numeral to integer"""
result = 0
index = 0
for numeral, integer in romanNumeralMap:
while s[index:index+len(numeral)] == numeral:
result += integer
index += len(numeral)
return result

@{body}

The pattern here is the same as @{code}toRoman@{body}. You iterate through your Roman numeral data structure (a tuple of tuples), and instead of matching the highest integer values as often as possible, you match the "highest" Roman numeral character strings as often as possible.

@{b}Example 14.10. How @{code}fromRoman@{body} works @{ub}

If you're not clear how @{code}fromRoman@{body} works, add a @{code}print@{body} statement to the end of the @{code}while@{body} loop:

@{code}

while s[index:index+len(numeral)] == numeral:
result += integer
index += len(numeral)
print 'found', numeral, 'of length', len(numeral), ', adding', integer
@{body}

@{code}

>>> import roman4
>>> roman4.fromRoman('MCMLXXII')
found M , of length 1, adding 1000
found CM , of length 2, adding 900
found L , of length 1, adding 50
found X , of length 1, adding 10
found X , of length 1, adding 10
found I , of length 1, adding 1
found I , of length 1, adding 1
1972
@{body}

@{b}Example 14.11. Output of @{code}romantest4.py@{body} against @{code}roman4.py@{body}@{ub}

