Two pieces of exciting news here. The first is that @{code}fromRoman@{body} works for good input, at least for all the known values you test.

The second is that the sanity check also passed. Combined with the known values tests, you can be reasonably sure that both @{code}toRoman@{body} and @{code}fromRoman@{body} work properly for all possible good values. (This is not guaranteed; it is theoretically possible that @{code}toRoman@{body} has a bug that produces the wrong Roman numeral for some particular set of inputs, @{i}and@{ui} that @{code}fromRoman@{body} has a reciprocal bug that produces the same wrong integer values for exactly that set of Roman numerals that @{code}toRoman@{body} generated incorrectly. Depending on your application and your requirements, this possibility may bother you; if so, write more comprehensive test cases until it doesn't bother you.)

